<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nominoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/851">851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nominoe was the first ruler of a united Brittany, a Breton noble and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> appointed as his representative there in 831. After Louis's death he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, defeated him at Ballon in 845 and made Brittany independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>. He died in 851 while campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> later honoured him as father of their nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nominoe.jpg" alt="Nominoe" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Tenniel / Joseph Swain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nom%C3%A9no%C3%AB_(Tenniel).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nominoe was the second son of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a> of Poher, and his elder brother was Count Riwallon of Poher. After a general rebellion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> was put down, an assembly was held at Ingelheim in May 831, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> probably appointed him to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a>, which meant almost all of Brittany. His power base was the Vannetais, and two charters call him Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>. He remained a staunch ally of Louis until the emperor's death in 840 and backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redon-abbey">Redon Abbey</a>.</p><p>The title Duke of Brittany is mainly a chronicler's invention of the tenth century. The emperor called him only fidelis or missus imperatoris, and Breton charters gave him many titles, among them master, prince and duke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> were at first friendly. In spring 841 Nominoe agreed to submit to him, and later that year he rebuffed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>. Breton soldiers took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a>. In summer 843, however, Lothair or his supporter Lambert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> persuaded him to change sides, and he became an enemy of Charles. In June 844 he raided Maine while Charles besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. At the synod of Yutz in October 844 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ordered him to renew his fealty to Charles. Lambert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-ii-of-aquitaine">Pepin II of Aquitaine</a> complied, but Nominoe did not, and Charles was defeated at the battle of Ballon on 22 November 845.</p><p>In summer 846 Charles marched into Brittany and made peace with Nominoe, and the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> calls him duke for the first time in this context. Nominoe soon resumed raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, probably with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>'s backing. In 849 he held a synod at Coitlouh that deposed five Breton bishops, those of Alet, Saint-Pol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a> and Dol, and replaced them with his own men. Later popes, among them Nicholas I, held the depositions invalid, and a Frankish synod of 850 accused him of simony. He also sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, and the Breton church thereby became independent in practice of its metropolitan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 850 Nominoe and Lambert raided Maine together and defeated the garrisons that Charles had placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. Nominoe died on 7 March 851 near Vendôme while ravaging the Nantais and Anjou, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redon-abbey">Redon Abbey</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a>, by his wife Argentaela, succeeded him. He founded a political tradition in Brittany that had not existed before, and his successors imitated Carolingian language in their charters. To Breton nationalists he is Tad ar Vro, father of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nominoe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brittany, the Armorican peninsula settled by migrants from south-western Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries, had formed a kingdom under <b>Nominoe</b>, Erispoe and Salomon in the ninth century, when its rulers fought the Carolingians and won the counties of Rennes and Nantes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Breton ruler <b>Nominoe</b> defeated the West Frankish king Charles the Bald at Ballon, near Bains-sur-Oust in eastern Brittany.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-22">22 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Broërec remained the border zone between the Bretons and the Franks until it was absorbed into the Kingdom of Brittany created by <b>Nominoe</b>, who was count of Vannes for Louis the Pious.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century Cornouaille was drawn into the unified Kingdom of Brittany under <b>Nominoe</b> and Erispoe.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornouaille">Cornouaille</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 9th century Domnonée was absorbed into the Kingdom of Brittany created by <b>Nominoe</b> and Erispoe.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis the Pious appointed the Breton noble <b>Nominoe</b> as his representative in Brittany in 831.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Vendôme, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.17,-1&amp;year=841" aria-label="Armorica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4527 478 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nominoe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4977 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4977 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.17,-1&amp;year=841">What was here in 841 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
